MODULES = {
"Networking fundamentals": {
"intro":"A network is a group of devices exchanging data using agreed rules called protocols.",
"why":"Networks let devices share information and services, from home Wi-Fi to the Internet.",
"topics":["LAN, WAN, MAN and PAN","OSI seven-layer model","TCP/IP model","Encapsulation and decapsulation","MAC addresses","IPv4 and IPv6","Public and private IP addresses","Subnetting and CIDR","Default gateway","Switches, routers, hubs and access points","VLANs"],
"details":"OSI layers: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation and Application. TCP/IP groups real Internet functions into Link, Internet, Transport and Application. Encapsulation adds control information as data moves down a sender stack; decapsulation removes it at the receiver.",
"lab":"Run `ipconfig /all` on Windows or `ip addr` on Linux. Identify your private IPv4 address, subnet mask and default gateway.",
"interview":"Explain how a switch differs from a router. What happens when a host sends traffic outside its subnet?","refs":["https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc791","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8200"]},
"Transport protocols":{"intro":"The transport layer provides communication between processes on different hosts.","why":"Applications need to distinguish conversations and choose delivery behaviour.","topics":["TCP","UDP","TCP three-way handshake","TCP four-way termination","SYN, ACK, FIN and RST flags","Sequence and acknowledgement numbers","Sliding window","Flow control","Congestion control","Retransmission","TCP header structure","UDP header structure","TCP vs UDP"],"details":"TCP is connection-oriented and provides an ordered byte stream with retransmission and flow/congestion control. A common setup is SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK. UDP sends independent datagrams without built-in delivery or ordering guarantees. Both use ports to identify application endpoints.","lab":"Capture your own browser traffic in Wireshark. Filter with `tcp.flags.syn == 1` and compare with `udp`.","interview":"Why might DNS use UDP? What does the TCP handshake establish? Differentiate flow and congestion control.","refs":["https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc768"]},
"Web protocols":{"intro":"HTTP is an application-layer request/response protocol used by browsers and APIs.","why":"It standardises how clients request resources and submit data.","topics":["HTTP","HTTPS","HTTP/1.1","HTTP/2","HTTP/3","GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE","HTTP headers","Cookies and sessions","Status codes","Request and response lifecycle","REST APIs"],"details":"A request includes method, target, version, headers and sometimes a body. A response includes status, headers and optional body. HTTPS is HTTP protected by TLS. HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP; HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 commonly use TCP.","lab":"Open browser developer tools → Network, reload a page and inspect method, status and headers.","interview":"Differentiate GET and POST. Explain 301 vs 302 and 401 vs 403.","refs":["https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114"]},
"Security protocols":{"intro":"Cryptographic protocols protect confidentiality, integrity and peer authentication.","why":"Without protection, traffic may be observed, modified or impersonated.","topics":["SSL and its deprecation","TLS 1.2","TLS 1.3","TLS handshake","Digital certificates","Certificate authorities","Public and private keys","Symmetric and asymmetric encryption","Hashing","HMAC","HTTPS security"],"details":"Modern HTTPS uses TLS. A certificate binds a public key to an identity and is validated through a trust chain. TLS 1.3 negotiates cryptographic parameters and establishes shared traffic keys. SSL is obsolete and should not be enabled.","lab":"Inspect TLS ClientHello and ServerHello metadata in Wireshark. Encrypted application payloads should not be assumed readable.","interview":"Compare encryption and hashing. What does a certificate authority do? Why avoid SSL?","refs":["https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5280"]},
"Network services":{"intro":"DNS, DHCP, ARP and ICMP provide essential network functions.","why":"They help devices find systems, obtain configuration and diagnose connectivity.","topics":["DNS","DHCP","ARP","ICMP","NTP","SNMP","DNS records","Recursive and iterative DNS","DHCP DORA","ARP request and reply","Ping and traceroute"],"details":"DNS maps names to records such as A and AAAA. DHCP commonly uses Discover, Offer, Request and Acknowledge (DORA). ARP resolves an IPv4 address to a link-layer address on the local link. ICMP supports diagnostics and error reporting.","lab":"Run `nslookup example.com`, `ipconfig /all` and `ping 1.1.1.1`; compare name resolution with IP connectivity.","interview":"Walk through DHCP DORA. Compare A and AAAA. When does a host use ARP?","refs":["https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1035","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2131","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc826"]},
"File transfer and email":{"intro":"File-transfer and email protocols define how clients exchange files and messages.","why":"Different protocols balance compatibility, security and message storage/retrieval.","topics":["FTP","FTPS","SFTP","TFTP","SSH","Telnet","SMTP","POP3","IMAP"],"details":"FTP traditionally separates control and data channels and does not encrypt credentials by itself. FTPS adds TLS; SFTP is file transfer over SSH, not FTP. SSH provides secure remote access; Telnet is plaintext. SMTP sends mail; IMAP and POP3 retrieve it.","lab":"Compare protocol documentation and explain why plaintext Telnet/FTP credentials are risky. Never transmit real credentials in a lab.","interview":"Differentiate FTPS and SFTP. Compare IMAP and POP3. Why is Telnet insecure?","refs":["https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4251","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5321"]},
"Routing and infrastructure":{"intro":"Routing moves packets between IP networks; infrastructure controls segmentation and access.","why":"Networks need scalable path selection, address translation and policy enforcement.","topics":["Static and dynamic routing","RIP","OSPF","BGP","NAT","PAT","IPv6 routing","VLAN tagging","IEEE 802.1Q","Firewalls","Proxies","VPNs"],"details":"Routers consult forwarding information to choose a next hop. OSPF is an interior link-state protocol; BGP exchanges reachability between autonomous systems. NAT rewrites addresses; PAT also uses transport ports. VLANs logically segment Layer 2 networks.","lab":"Draw two LANs connected by a router. Trace source/destination IP and next-hop MAC at each link using a simulator you control.","interview":"Compare OSPF and BGP. Explain NAT vs PAT. What does VLAN segmentation solve?","refs":["https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2328","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4271"]},
"SOC Analyst training":{"intro":"A SOC monitors systems, investigates alerts and coordinates defensive response.","why":"Continuous monitoring helps detect suspicious activity and reduce incident impact.","topics":["SOC architecture","SIEM fundamentals","Log analysis","Security event monitoring","Alert triage","Incident response lifecycle","Network traffic investigation","DNS investigation","Suspicious IP analysis","Authentication log analysis","Firewall log analysis","MITRE ATT&CK fundamentals","Threat intelligence fundamentals"],"details":"A SIEM collects and correlates endpoint, identity, firewall and application telemetry. Analysts validate alerts, scope affected assets, preserve evidence, assess severity, escalate and document actions. ATT&CK maps observed behaviours to techniques; a match alone is not proof of compromise.","lab":"Review sample authentication logs. Group failed logins by account, source IP and time window; list evidence needed before declaring an incident.","interview":"Describe alert triage. Differentiate event, alert and incident. Investigate repeated failed logins.","refs":["https://attack.mitre.org/","https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"]},
"Penetration testing":{"intro":"Penetration testing is an authorised assessment that identifies and validates security weaknesses.","why":"Controlled testing helps organisations prioritise remediation before attackers exploit weaknesses.","topics":["Ethical hacking principles","Written authorisation and scope","Reconnaissance","Footprinting","Nmap","Port scanning","Service enumeration","Vulnerability assessment","Web application security","OWASP Top 10","Burp Suite fundamentals","Wireshark","Reporting and remediation"],"details":"Begin with written permission, scope, timing, permitted techniques and contacts. Phases include planning, discovery, validation, impact analysis, reporting and retesting. Findings should include evidence, impact, likelihood and remediation.","lab":"On your own computer, run `nmap -sV 127.0.0.1` only if installed. Never scan public or third-party targets without explicit written authorisation.","interview":"Why is scope important? Differentiate vulnerability scanning and penetration testing. What makes a useful finding report?","refs":["https://nmap.org/book/man.html","https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/"]}
}
